  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs expected, smoke from wildfires across Southern Canada into \nnorthern Minnesota has moved into the area, especially across\nCentral NY. This has significantly limited solar heating and in\nfact, temperatures have fallen a few degrees over the course of\nthe afternoon during a time that they would normally be rising.\nThat being said, there is a very small corridor of lighter smoke\nconcentration across the Wyoming Valley in PA and this has \nallowed enough instability for some thunderstorms to form. These\nare pulse thunderstorms, but do have the potentially to quickly\nbecome severe before also quickly falling apart.\n\nThursday will be another day where thick smoke will likely\nsignificantly limit temperatures. Therefore went several degrees\nbelow the NBM for the forecast, which resulted in forecast highs\nin the upper 70s to mid 80s. It is certainly possible that the\nhigh temperatures could be several degrees lower than this if\nsmoke concentrations remain thick enough.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA trough digs into the Northeast late Friday into the weekend \nwith a broad surface low moving through. This will likely bring \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms to the region. 0-6 km shear\nis between 30 and 40 knots so a few storms in the afternoon \nafter day time heating may become severe. We remain on the \neastern edge of the large ridge in the Western US so remain in a\nfavorable severe pattern into early next week as well.",
